**The error:** A segmentation fault means your program touched memory the OS didn't give it.

**The cause:** `int *p;` declares a pointer but does not initialise it. `p` contains whatever garbage was on the stack at that address - it does not point at any `int` you own. `*p = 5` then writes 4 bytes to that random address. If it happens to be outside your process's mapped pages, the OS kills you. If it happens to be *inside*, you silently corrupt something else, which is worse.

**The fix - two options depending on what you meant:**

```c
// Option 1: point it at memory you already have
int x;
int *p = &x;        // p now holds the address of a real int
*p = 5;
printf("%d\n", *p); // 5

// Option 2: allocate memory on the heap
#include <stdlib.h>
int *p = malloc(sizeof(*p));
if (p == NULL) {             // malloc can fail - always check
    fprintf(stderr, "allocation failed\n");
    return 1;
}
*p = 5;
printf("%d\n", *p);
free(p);
p = NULL;                    // avoid a dangling pointer
```

